---
<DocsLayout title="Reading stats" description="Designed stats pages — reading time, habits, your library, your ratings and every session — plus your own boards, built from real session data.">
  <SectionHero section="features" />
  <h1>Reading stats</h1>
  <p>
    Tome's stats are built from real session data — every minute logged by the web reader
    and every session the TomeSync KOReader plugin reports. No estimates from page counts, no
    fudge factors, no guessing. If a chart is empty, it's because the data isn't there yet.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hover (or tap) any bar, cell, dot or row for its exact value; books, series and authors are
    links. The page has seven tabs: <strong>Overview</strong>, <strong>Habits</strong>, <strong>Library</strong>, <strong>Taste</strong>, <strong>Timeline</strong>, <strong>Sessions</strong>, and — when
    you have any — <strong>Boards</strong>, your own layouts from the earlier customisable
    dashboard. The range pills (7d, 30d, 90d, 1y, All, or a custom range) apply to every tab that
    depends on a period; sections that are all-time say so in their heading.
  </p>
  <DocsMeta />

  <h2>How the pages behave</h2>
  <p>
    Each page has a fixed layout. It draws its outline before the numbers arrive and fills it in
    place, so nothing jumps around while it loads. Changing the range keeps the current numbers
    on screen, dimmed, with a thin line under the header until the new ones are there. The tab
    and range you were on are remembered in this browser; looking around never changes anything
    you saved. The image button next to the range saves the page you are looking at as a PNG.
  </p>

  <h2>Your own boards</h2>
  <p>
    Before the designed pages, Stats was a dashboard of tiles on a drag-and-resize grid. Every
    board you saved there is kept, unchanged, in the same place on the server. Boards you
    customised (moved, resized, added or removed tiles, changed a tile's settings, renamed) appear
    under the <strong>Boards</strong> tab and work exactly as before: <strong>Edit</strong> on the
    board row, drag and resize, the tile gallery, duplicate, import and export as JSON, Reset.
  </p>
  <p>
    A saved board that is still exactly one of the default boards a Tome release shipped is not
    listed — the designed pages replace it. It is only hidden, never deleted or rewritten. If none
    of your boards differs from a default, the Boards tab is not shown at all; <strong>New
    board</strong> in the page's <strong>⋯</strong> menu creates one and brings the tab back. Boards
    are saved only when you edit them, so they still follow you to every device.
  </p>
  <p>
    To use the grid without customising anything first, turn on <strong>Show Boards</strong> under
    Settings › Appearance. The Boards tab is then always there and lists every board, the defaults
    included, editable as before; the setting follows your account. Turned off, the Boards tab and
    the <strong>⋯</strong> menu are hidden, even if you have boards of your own; your boards are kept
    and are back when you turn it on again. Until you first touch the switch, it shows on exactly
    when you have a board of your own.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Two writers, one table. The web reader and the <a href={withBase("/docs/koreader")}>TomeSync plugin</a> both POST to <code>reading_sessions</code>: start
    time, end time, pages turned, device. The stats endpoints aggregate from there. The <code>UserBookStatus</code> table provides finished-book counts (<code>status = "read"</code>)
    and the <code>TomeSyncPosition</code> table provides current-progress percentages.
  </p>

  <Callout variant="info" title="The 4-hour rollover">
    A session that starts at 02:30 counts against the previous calendar day, not the new one. So
    bedtime reads don't fragment your streak, and "a day of reading" matches what feels like a
    day to you. Implemented in <code>backend/services/streaks.py</code> as a tz-aware date
    bucket.
  </Callout>

  <Callout variant="warning" title="Time zones matter">
    The frontend sends your browser's <code>getTimezoneOffset</code> with every stats request.
    Hour-of-day buckets, streak boundaries, and daily aggregates all use <em>your</em> local time.
    Two users in different time zones see different "today."
  </Callout>

  <p>
    Reading from before Tome can come along too: <strong>Settings → Import reading
    history</strong> takes a Goodreads or StoryGraph CSV export, matches it against your
    library (ISBN first, then title/author) with a full preview, and only ever fills gaps —
    statuses, ratings, reviews, and finish dates you already have are never overwritten.
  </p>

  <h2>Fixing a runaway session</h2>
  <p>
    A device that never actually went to sleep can sync a session hours longer than the reading
    it contains — 15 minutes of pages, then a night of idle screen. The <a href={withBase("/docs/koreader")}>TomeSync plugin</a> prevents this at the source with its
    idle time cap, but sessions from older plugin builds (or other clients) are sanity-checked
    on arrival: anything implausibly long, or averaging many minutes per page turn, is flagged
    with a warning icon and raises a bell notification.
  </p>
  <p>
    Any session list lets you fix one: <strong>trim</strong> (the scissors icon) shortens the
    session to what you actually read, while delete removes it entirely. The trim editor
    pre-fills a suggestion — the session's own page turns priced at your median seconds-per-page,
    computed from your recent plausible sessions so the runaways can't skew their own baseline.
    It's only ever a pre-fill; nothing is changed until you confirm. Session lists live in the
    <strong>Sessions</strong> tab (sortable longest-first), in each row of the Library page's book
    table, and in a collapsible
    <strong>Sessions</strong> section under Reading Stats on every book page; a
    <strong>Recent sessions</strong> tile on one of your own boards works the same way.
  </p>

  <Callout variant="info" title="Nothing else is touched">
    Trimming rewrites exactly two fields on that one session — its duration and end time. Page
    turns, progress, start time, device, other sessions, and imported KOReader history are all
    untouched, and the previous duration is recorded in the audit log, so a trim is never a
    silent loss of data.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Imported KOReader history in the session lists</h2>
  <p>
    Reading synced from KOReader's own statistics database — including everything read before
    Tome existed, if you enabled the <a href={withBase("/docs/koreader")}>reading-history
    backfill</a> — appears in the same session lists as everything else. The raw per-page
    timings are grouped into sittings the way KOReader itself thinks about sessions (a gap of
    more than 30 minutes starts a new one), and each sitting shows as a row tagged <strong>imported</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Imported sittings can be deleted individually — the fix for a book opened by accident,
    whose stray minutes used to be removable only by wiping the book's entire imported
    history. Deleting one removes exactly that sitting's page timings and the totals drop with
    it. They can't be trimmed: KOReader already caps idle time at the source, so there is
    nothing to cut. Two labels tell the rest of the story: when imported history describes a
    sitting the plugin also recorded as a live session, that live session stays listed with a <strong>not counted</strong> tag while the imported row carries the totals — nothing is
    ever counted twice, and nothing silently disappears. The rule is per sitting, not per
    book: reading the same book on a second device that never synced its history still
    counts through its live sessions. The small info icon next to every
    session list explains all labels in place.
  </p>
  <p>
    For getting at one stray day quickly, the <strong>Activity</strong> chart on a book's
    Reading Stats block is clickable: each bar opens a popup with the sittings that started on
    that day, with the same trim and delete actions — no scrolling the full log.
  </p>
  <Callout variant="info" title="Deleted history stays deleted">
    The device only ever uploads statistics newer than its last sync, so a deleted sitting is
    not re-imported on the next connection. New reading syncs and counts as normal.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <div class="docs-shot-bare">
    <ThemedShot client:load name="stats-overview" alt="The Stats Overview: reading time with the change against the previous period, four figures, weekly bars, books in progress and finished" />
  </div>
  <p>
    The "where am I right now?" page. At the top, your <strong>reading time</strong> in the
    range with the change against the period just before it, and four figures beside it:
    sessions (with the average length), pages (with the daily average), books finished (with the
    count for the previous period) and your current streak (since when, and whether it is your
    longest). Under them, the reading time as bars: one per day for ranges up to 31 days, one
    per week above that, with the running week lighter, your average marked and your best day of
    the range noted in the corner. At <strong>1y</strong> and <strong>All</strong> the corner also
    names your most active month of the twelve the chart shows, with its reading time.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you have <strong>reading goals</strong>, one row of up to four follows, each with its
    progress and pace; <strong>All goals</strong> opens the <a href="#goals">Goals</a> page.
    Without goals, a quiet line at the end of the page sets one up.
  </p>
  <p>
    The streak is in local-with-rollover days, with a one-day grace: if you haven't read yet
    today, the streak still shows yesterday's value instead of dropping to zero. Once midnight
    (plus rollover) passes without a session, the streak breaks.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reading now</strong> — the books you're reading (up to three, with a link to all of
      them in the library when there are more), with cover, progress, an estimate of the days left
      at your recent pace, and when you last read them. At 99 % a <strong>Mark as read</strong>
      button appears. Progress is the furthest
      of your synced position and the furthest page in imported KOReader history; the same value
      is used everywhere in Stats.</li>
    <li><strong>Finished</strong> — the covers of the books you finished in the range, newest first.</li>
    <li><strong>Where the time went</strong> — your top five books by reading time; <strong>All
      books</strong> jumps to the full table on Library.</li>
    <li><strong>When you read</strong> — your reading by hour and weekday, with the peak in words.</li>
    <li><strong>The year</strong> — a 365-day grid of daily reading time. Its five shades are cut at
      your own quantiles, and the legend prints where each step starts.</li>
    <li><strong>All time</strong> — the end of the page is your lifetime: first the totals (reading
      time with the average per reading day, sessions with their average length, pages, books
      finished out of those you started, and active days, the days with any reading), then the
      records: longest session, biggest reading day, most pages in a day, longest daily streak and
      longest weekly streak.</li>
  </ul>
  <Callout variant="tip" title="Shelving a book you've set aside">
    Every book has reading-status pills — <strong>Unread / Reading / Read</strong>, plus a fourth
    set slightly apart: <strong>Shelved</strong>. Shelving a book pulls it off Currently Reading,
    Series Progress, and the completion stats, but <em>keeps your exact position</em> (progress and
    CFI), so picking it back up later resumes where you left off. It's the middle ground between
    leaving a stalled book cluttering your "reading" list and marking it <strong>Unread</strong>
    — which clears your progress entirely. Find shelved titles again with the <strong>Shelved</strong> filter in the library. Reading the book again on any device automatically moves it back to
    Reading.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="goals">Goals</h2>
  <p>
    The "am I on track?" page. It does not follow the range: every goal counts its own calendar
    period (this week, this month, this year, or up to the date of a finish-by goal), so the
    header reads <strong>This week, month and year</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each goal is a card: its name, how far you are against the target, and what it takes. Goals
    of a month or a year show a bar with a tick where you should be today, and say how far
    ahead or behind of that pace you are ("5 ahead of pace", "2 behind pace"), how many are to
    go, how many days are left and, once a quarter of the period has passed, what you are on
    course for. Day goals and reading days show the week as seven marks: a filled dot for a day
    reached, a dim dot for a day missed, an outline for the days to come and a ring that fills
    as you read today. The same cards are on Home.
  </p>
  <p>
    Click a card to see the goal in detail under the cards (the page remembers your choice):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Minutes or pages a day, reading days a week</strong>: this week large, the last
      12 weeks day by day, and how often you reached it in the last 90 days, your best run and
      your current one.</li>
    <li><strong>Minutes or pages a week</strong>: this week day by day against an even share of
      the target, and the last 12 weeks against the target.</li>
    <li><strong>Books a month or a year</strong>: the period so far against the pace line, the
      projection to its end, and past months or years. When most of a month's finish dates share
      one day (books marked read in bulk), a note says so: those bars show when books were
      marked read, not when they were read.</li>
    <li><strong>Finish by a date</strong>: the volumes read and to go, the reading time left at
      your own time per volume of that series (or the word count at your reading speed), the
      minutes a day that takes against what you read a day over the last 30 days, and the road
      to the date.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>New goal</strong> offers eight kinds: books a year or a month, minutes or pages a day
    or a week, <strong>reading days a week</strong> (a day counts as it does for your streak) and
    <strong>finish by a date</strong> (a book or a series, by the end of the month, the end of the
    year or any day you pick). The goals you already have show their progress and are not
    offered twice; count goals can be limited to one book type under <strong>Counts</strong>.
    Targets are suggested from your own reading: Steady, Keep it up and Stretch are what you
    reached on three days in four, half your days and one day in four over the last 90 days (12
    weeks for weekly goals), each with how often you reached it and what it means ("at 60 min a
    day you'd finish about 3.7 books a month"). Book goals are suggested from your reading time
    too, at your usual time per finished book, so books marked read in bulk do not inflate them.
    Without enough history the dialog offers a few fixed targets.
  </p>
  <p>
    Edit and delete sit beside the selected goal's name. A deleted goal can be brought back with
    <strong>Undo</strong> for a little while. A goal's past periods are judged by the target it had
    then; periods before you set it are shown but not counted as met or missed.
  </p>
  <Callout variant="info" title="When a week starts">
    Weeks run Monday to Sunday unless you choose Sunday under <strong>Settings › Language ›
    Week starts on</strong>. Goals follow it, and so do the weekly streak, the calendar
    and the charts by weekday on the web. Days are Tome's reading days: a day ends at 4 a.m., as it does for streaks.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Habits</h2>
  <p>The "when and how do I read?" page.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>When you read</strong> — the hour × weekday grid at full width, the weekday totals in
      its right margin and the hour totals under it. The shades are per reader: your darkest cell
      is your peak.</li>
    <li><strong>Last 14 days</strong> — one row per reading day, including days without reading, on a
      track from 04:00 to 04:00 so a late-night sitting sits at the end of the evening it belongs
      to. Each bar is a sitting at its start time; the day's total is on the right.</li>
    <li><strong>Sessions</strong> — the average sitting, sittings per reading day, and the longest
      sitting in the range. <strong>Session length</strong> sorts the range's sittings into five
      lengths, from under 15 minutes to over two hours.</li>
    <li><strong>Pace</strong> — pages per minute, a dot per reading day and a 7-day mean, with a
      sentence comparing your last seven reading days to the whole range. Both come from the same
      daily pages and minutes, so the sentence and the chart always agree; the
      change between the two ("up 27 %") is worked out from the two printed figures. Under the
      chart, one line gives each format's pages a minute and its share of the time once you read
      in more than one format.</li>
    <li><strong>Reading speed</strong> — all time: your words per minute (word count over the time
      actually recorded, from finished books with a word count and at least five minutes of
      reading) with your fastest and slowest books. A second line adds up the words in every
      finished book that has a word count.</li>
    <li><strong>The last 12 months</strong> — reading hours and books finished per month, as two
      aligned rows.</li>
    <li><strong>Reading character</strong> — your reading personality as an archetype plus the trait
      spectrums that produced it (short reads vs. long epics, focused vs. eclectic, early bird
      vs. night owl, …).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Library</h2>
  <p>The "what is on my shelves, and what have I made of it?" page. Mostly all time.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your library</strong> — books owned, read, in progress and wanted. Under In
      progress: how many books you have started (marked reading or read) and the share of them
      you have finished.</li>
    <li><strong>Series</strong> — the series you've started, how far you are, and the next volume:
      the volume after the highest one you've read or are reading, and whether it is on your
      shelf. Finished series collapse to a row of covers.</li>
    <li><strong>By type</strong> — per book type: owned, read (out of everything you own), finish
      rate (out of what you started) and reading time in the range.</li>
    <li><strong>Languages</strong> — reading time per language, all time, once you read in at least
      two.</li>
    <li><strong>Authors</strong> — your ten most-read authors in the range by reading time.</li>
    <li><strong>Backlog</strong> — how long a pile would take at your own pace, in total and per
      type. The pile is chosen in the heading: your want-to-read list (the default), everything
      unread, a library, or a shelf; the choice is remembered in this browser.</li>
  </ul>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hours</strong> come from the book's word count at your measured words per minute.
      Books without a word count — CBZ and PDF — fall back to your average time per finished
      book of that type. A book Tome can't place either way is counted as "not estimated yet"
      rather than guessed.</li>
    <li><strong>Days</strong> come from your minutes per day over the last 30 days, or the last
      90 when the past month was quiet. No recent reading, no day figure.</li>
  </ul>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Book length</strong>, <strong>Published</strong> and <strong>Time to finish</strong> —
      how long the books you finish are, when they were published, and how many days finishing
      usually takes. Time to finish counts from a book's first reading day to its finish, and
      stops at the last reading day when the book was marked read later (a bulk "mark as read"
      doesn't stretch it).</li>
    <li><strong>Added to the library</strong> — books added per month by type. A bulk import shows as
      a broken bar with its number instead of flattening every other month.</li>
    <li><strong>All books by reading time</strong> — every book you read in the range, sortable by
      time, sessions or pages. The arrow at the end of a row opens that book's sessions in place,
      with the same trim and delete actions as the Sessions tab.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Taste</h2>
  <p>Your ratings, all time.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ratings</strong> — your average rating and how many books you rated, beside the
      distribution of stars.</li>
    <li><strong>Highest rated</strong> as a shelf of covers, and the lowest rated as a short list.</li>
    <li><strong>Series</strong> — the series you rated, with how many books each has.</li>
    <li><strong>Does time spent match the rating?</strong> — reading time against stars, one lane per
      star value, with the median time of your 5-star and 4-star books in a sentence.</li>
    <li><strong>Ratings over time</strong> — every rating as a dot (identical ratings on one day are
      one dot with a count) and the mean of your last five as a step line.</li>
    <li><strong>By type</strong> appears once books of at least two types are rated, and
      <strong>Highlights</strong> once you have five highlights.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Timeline</h2>
  <p>
    Your reading life as a Gantt chart. One lane per series (standalones get their own),
    named in a frozen rail on the left; every book is a bar spanning its first to last
    active reading day, with the volume number at the bar's start. Inside each bar, every
    day you actually read is a tick whose intensity encodes that day's minutes — scaled
    against your 90th-percentile reading day, so one marathon doesn't flatten every normal
    evening. Gaps inside a bar are days the book rested; gaps between bars are pauses
    between volumes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The data is lifetime and fully reconciled: live sessions and imported KOReader history
    merge with the same page-stats-win-per-book rule as the rest of stats, bucketed by the
    canonical 4-hour-rollover reading day. Lanes sort by most recent activity and the view
    opens at today, so the top-right corner is what you're reading now — pan left for
    history. Zoom from a year-at-a-glance down to month detail; hovering a bar answers at
    day resolution ("15 May · 22m", or "no reading" on a gap day) alongside the book's
    totals, and clicking opens the book. On phones the rail narrows and the first tap
    inspects while a second tap navigates.
  </p>
  <p>
    Timeline is its own tab and renders full-bleed — edge to edge, viewport-tall. It ignores
    the range. The ribbon is also available as a tile for your own boards.
  </p>

  <h2>Sessions</h2>
  <p>
    Every reading session, grouped by calendar day with the day's counted total, newest first.
    It opens on <strong>Counted</strong> — one row per sitting, exactly what the totals count.
    <strong>Not counted</strong> lists the live device sessions whose sitting imported KOReader
    history also describes, and <strong>All</strong> shows both. Each row can be trimmed or deleted
    as described above. The log ignores the range, so the range pills are hidden on this tab.
  </p>

  <h2>Page insights on a book</h2>
  <p>
    On each book's page, a <strong>Reading intensity</strong> strip shows where your time went across
    the book — page by page — with a real &ldquo;X of Y pages&rdquo; read and a note when you've
    re-read stretches.
  </p>
  <div class="docs-shot-bare">
    <ThemedShot client:load name="book-intensity" alt="Reading intensity strip on a book page: per-page dwell across the book, pages read, and re-read stretches" />
  </div>
  <p>
    The same per-book view carries a full <strong>reading log</strong>: every session with device,
    duration and pages, your day-by-day progress, and a per-book time total. You can <strong>log a session by hand</strong> (paper reading, or a device that wasn't synced) and <strong>export</strong> a book's log to CSV or JSON.
  </p>

  <h2>Charts that aren't here yet</h2>
  <p>
    Things explicitly considered and not built:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Per-chapter pace</strong> within a book — too noisy for the current data shape; sessions cross chapters and chapter boundaries aren't tracked.</li>
    <li><strong>Sleep-quality correlation</strong> — we have the data (late-night session detection) but no second data source to correlate against.</li>
    <li><strong>Multi-user leaderboards</strong> — stats are per-user only by design. Tome isn't trying to gamify reading across a household.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>API access</h2>
  <p>
    All the data behind these charts is reachable via the API. Useful for one-off scripts:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>GET /api/stats?days=30&amp;tz_offset=-120</code> — the main bundle (everything on this page).</li>
    <li><code>GET /api/stats/completion-estimates</code> — projected finish dates for in-progress books.</li>
    <li><code>GET /api/stats/habits</code>, <code>/api/stats/library</code>, <code>/api/stats/highlights</code> — what the Habits, Library and Taste pages add to the main bundle.</li>
    <li><code>GET /api/stats/sessions</code> — paged session list, newest first (or longest first); <code>counted=counted|not_counted|all</code> filters it and <code>day_totals=true</code> adds each calendar day's counted total.</li>
    <li><code>GET /api/stats/timeline</code> — lifetime per-book reading spans and active days (the Timeline tab).</li>
    <li><code>DELETE /api/stats/sessions/{`{id}`}</code> — delete a misfire.</li>
    <li><code>GET /api/stats/dashboard</code> / <code>PUT /api/stats/dashboard</code> — your saved boards (opaque JSON, 256&nbsp;KB cap), written only when you edit a board or flip Show Boards.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Authenticate with an <a href={withBase("/docs/api-tokens")}>API token</a> or a JWT. Times are UTC in the
    payload; bucketing happens server-side using the <code>tz_offset</code> you pass.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
